<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Granicus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/334-bce">334 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Granicus · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-alexander-the-great">Wars of Alexander the Great</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Granicus was fought in 334 BCE at the Granicus River in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, the first major victory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> over the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a>. It opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> it was the first of the defeats that led to its fall.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-granicus.jpg" alt="Battle of the Granicus" width="320" height="145" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Le Brun / Abraham Genoels · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Le_Brun,_Le_Passage_du_Granique,_1665.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After winning the Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> forced most Greek states into the Hellenic League, whose aim was war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> to avenge the Persian invasion of Greece in 480 BCE. The Persian empire had been in crisis since the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a> in 338 BCE, and in spring 336 BCE Philip sent an expeditionary corps to the western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>.</p><p>In early spring 334 BCE Alexander marched to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> with around 32,000 infantry and 5,000 cavalry. Darius left the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a>, perhaps because Alexander had not yet proved himself abroad. The satraps, led most probably by Arsites, satrap of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespontine-phrygia">Hellespontine Phrygia</a>, and including Spithridates and Memnon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, were camped near Zeleia when Alexander crossed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The battle was fought in May 334 BCE at the crossing of the Granicus, now called the Biga River, on the road from Abydus to Dascylium. Arrian's account, the longest, describes Alexander's army as 18,100 men, and gives the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> almost 20,000 Greek mercenary infantry and 20,000 cavalry. Diodorus and Justin give far higher figures, which modern historians regard as exaggerated.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>'s account Alexander first sent Amyntas against the Persian left, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> held the higher bank and threw back the attack with javelins. Alexander then charged with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/companion-cavalry">Companion cavalry</a> and fought personally against Persian nobles, among them Rhosaces and Spithridates. The Persian cavalry broke, the Foot Companions crossed the river and the Persian infantry routed, except the Greek mercenaries, who withdrew to higher ground and fought hard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> gives 115 Macedonian dead. Arsites escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> but later killed himself after being blamed for the defeat. Arrian says 1,000 cavalry were killed and 2,000 Greek mercenaries taken prisoner, and Alexander sent the prisoners to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> to work the land as slaves, because they had fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> against fellow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>.</p><p>Alexander sent 300 suits of Persian armour to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> as an offering to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, with an inscription that left out the Spartans. He also set up at Dion a bronze group by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysippus">Lysippus</a> of twenty-five of his companions who had died in the first cavalry charge; it was later taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-macedonicus">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Macedonicus</a> in 146 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> were forced onto the defensive in the cities they still held. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> surrendered to Alexander, and Miletus, which resisted, was besieged.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Granicus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The decisive military collapse came only with Alexander the Great's campaigns (334–330 BCE), when a tactically superior Macedonian army defeated Persian forces at the <b>Granicus</b>, Issus and Gaugamela in rapid succession.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His conquest of the Achaemenid Persian Empire included victories at the <b>Granicus</b> (334 BCE), Issus (333 BCE) and Gaugamela (331 BCE), the sieges of Tyre and Gaza (332 BCE), and the defeat of Porus at the Hydaspes (326 BCE), successes in pitched battle and in siege warfare alike.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
